title Kumara PQ seated — SLH-DSA the most-Lindy path, after an Aug 2026 news pass
prompt Keaton asked to choose the most Lindy path for the 21st century given what we know in August 2026, searching the past year of research first to be sure
think Ran a web search pass on 2025-2026 lattice cryptanalysis and NIST PQC migration guidance before committing a durable choice — measurement beats memory on a century-scale decision.
think Found the decisive signal: lattice is the assumption currently moving — an Oct 2025 quantum-sieving result cut the key lattice-attack exponent ~8% (0.3098 to 0.2846), and CRYPTO 2026 (opening Aug 17) carries three deployed-signature cryptanalysis papers including lattice-signature key recovery from signs. Hash-based SLH-DSA (FIPS 205) is the industry-designated long-lived-root / thirty-year-guarantee tool; hybrid-by-default is a transition posture for interop-bound orgs, which greenfield Kumara is not.
think This revised my prior turn's lean toward an SLH-DSA + ML-DSA hybrid — that assumed both assumptions were comparably stable; the evidence shows lattice is the one eroding. A Lindy century-root must not anchor to the moving assumption.
obs Seated Kumara v1 = SLH-DSA (SPHINCS+) on SHAKE-256, pure hash-based — security reduces to the hash alone, making the just-seated SHA-3 preference load-bearing (the identity is itself SHAKE-256).
obs Seated algorithm agility — the identity format carries a scheme tag + version so a second leg or migration accretes later without breaking the identity model (accrete-never-break applied to crypto); the lattice co-signature is deferred, not baked in, losing nothing thanks to agility.
obs Keaton delegated the choice ("choose the most Lindy path"), so seating v1 is authorized; the actual keygen and signing with the identity key stays the custody gate. The loop's crux is SHA3-512/SHAKE-256 in Rye, then the SLH-DSA build round, parity vs NIST FIPS 205 KATs.
